<section xml:id="section-fractions">
  <title>Fields of Fractions</title>
  <p>
    We've completed the main piece of ring theory for the course:
    understanding how primes and divisibility extend to rings other
    than the integers. However, I have a few more topics in ring
    theory that I want to cover before. The first is the idea of a
    field of fractions.
  </p>
  <p>
    The motivation here is the relationship between <m>\ZZ</m> and
    <m>\QQ</m>. As a ring, <m>\ZZ</m> has very few units: only
    <m>1</m> and <m>-1</m>. However, the larger ring <m>\QQ</m> is a
    field where everything is invertible. By moving from the integers
    to the rational numbers, it's reasonable to say that all of the
    integers become invertible. 
  </p>
  <p>
    I can think of this relationship as a construction. What is a
    rational number? It's ratio of integers with a non-zero
    denominator, with addition and multiplication defined for
    fractions. It also comes with some identifications:
    <m>\frac{1}{2}</m> is the same number as <m>\frac{2}{4}</m> or
    <m>\sqrt{3}{6}</m> and so on. In this way, the rational are build
    out of the integers by making fractions. Can this process be
    generalized? It can!
  </p>
  <proposition>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m>R</m> be a domain (commutative and with unity by
        default). There exists a minimal field <m>F</m> along with an
        injective ring homomorphism <m>R \rightarrow F</m>. (Minimal
        means that <m>F</m> is the smallest field for which such an
        injection exist.) <m>F</m> is called the <term>field of
        fractions<term> of <m>R</m>. 
      </p>
    </statement>
    <proof>
      <p>
        The proof works by actually constructing the field. As a set,
        I define <m>F</m> in two steps. First I define a set <m>A</m>
        <me>
          A = \{ \frac{a}{b} | a \in R \text{ and } b \in R\{0\} \}</m>
        </me>
        This the set of fractions formed from the elements of the
        ring, with zero explicitly excluded from the denominator. But
        fractions are not enough: I know from the rational numbers
        that different fractions can represent the same numbers.
        Therefore, I define an equivalence relation on this set. 
        <me>
          \frac{a}{b} \sim \frac{c}{d} \iff ad = bc
        </me>
      </p>
      <p>
        I need to prove that this is an equivalence relation, so I go
        through the properties. First, reflextive. <m>\frac{a}{b} \sim
        \frac{a}{b}</m> because <m>ab = ab</m>. That works.
      </p>
      <p>
        Next, symmetric. This follows from the commutativity of
        multiplication. The condition for <m>\frac{a}{b} \sim
        \frac{c}{d}</m> is <m>ad = bc</m>. The condition for
        <m>\frac{c}{d} \sim \frac{a}{b}</m> is <m>da = cb</m>. Since
        multiplication is commutative, these two conditions are
        equivalent.
      </p>
      <p>
        Finally, transitivity. Assume <m>\frac{a}{b} \sim
        \frac{c}{d}</m> and <m>\frac{c}{d} \sim \frac{e}{f}</m>. This
        means that I know two equations: <m>ad = bc</m> and <m>cf =
        de</m>. Then consider this expression.
        <me>
          adf
        </me>
        In this expression, I can use the first equation to replace
        <m>ad</m>. 
        <me>
          adf = bcf
        </me>
        Similarly, I can use the second equation to replace <m>cf</m>.
        <me>
          adf = bcf = bde
        </me>
        Now I have <m>d</m> in the first and third expressions. Since
        this is a domain (there are no zero divisors), cancellation is
        valid even though <m>d</m> may not be a unit. 
        <me>
          adf = bde \implies af = be 
        </me>
        This is precisely the expression that proves <m>\frac{ab} \sim
        \frac{e}{f}</m>. I have satisfied all three properties, so
        this is an equivalent relation.
      </p>
      <p>
        Then I define <m>F</m> to be the set quotient of <m>A</m>
        under this equivalence relation: <m>F</m> is the set of
        fractions where <sq>similar fractions</sq> are treated as the
        same number. That's all good, but I still need to prove that
        <m>F</m> is a field, has the necessary injection, and is
        minimal. 
      </p>
      <p>
        The ring operations defined on <m>F</m> are as follows. First,
        <me>
          \frac{a}{b} + \frac{c}{d} = \frac{ad + bc}{bd} \hspace{1cm} 
          \frac{a}{b} \frac{c}{d} = \frac{ac}{bd} 
        </me>
        I'll leave it to you to verify that these are well-define on
        equivalence classes<mdash/>the proofs here are basically a
        review of grade-school fractions and the fact that they work
        properly. The element <m>\frac{0}{1}</m> is the additive
        identity. Simiarly, the distribution property hold for these
        two opeartions.
      </p>
      <p>
        The element <m>\frac{1}{1}</m> is the identity for
        multiplication in this ring, and the inverse of
        <m>\frac{a}{b}</m> is <m>\frac{b}{a}</m>. Both of these are
        immediate to verify. 
      </p>
      <p>
        For this to be a field, all non-zero elements have to have an
        inverse. This is, again, almost immediate. If <m>a \neq 0</m>,
        then <m>\frac{b}{a}</m> is in the ring and is an inverse to
        <m>\frac{a}{b}</m>. This shows that all non-zero elements of
        this ring are invertible: this is a field.
      </p>
      <p>
        Next, the injective homomorphism: <m>i: R \rightarrow F</m>.
        Here is the definition.
        <me>
          i(r) = \frac{r}{1}
        </me>
        I'll leave it to you to verify the homomorphism
        properties, which are again just ordinary fraction arithmetic.
        To determine injectivitiy, I calculate the kernel. Assume that
        <m>\frac{r}{1} \sim \frac{0}{1}</m>; that is, the image of
        <m>r</m> is in the same equivalence class as the additive
        identity. This equation for this equivalence is <m>r1 = 1\cdot
        0</m> which means <m>r = 0</m>. Therefore, the kernel is only
        the zero element in <m>R</m>, which means the map is
        injective. 
      </p>
      <p>
        Finally, the minimality. Assume that <m>G<m> is any other
        field such that there is an injective homomorphism <m>\phi: R
        \rightarrow G</m>. I can then define a homomorphism <m>\psi: F
        \rightarrow F</m> as follows.
        <me>
          \psi \left( \frac{a}{b} \right) = \phi(a) \left( \phi(b)
          \right)^{-1}
        </me>
        Again, I'll leave it to you to verify the homomorphism
        properties and the fact that this is well-defined on
        equivalence classes. I want to know the kernel of this map.
        The output is zero only is <m>\phi(a) = 0</m>. But <m>\phi</m>
        is injective, so it has a trivial kernel, which means that
        <m>a = 0</m>. The input is then <m>\frac{0}{b}</m> which is
        equivalent to <m>\farc{0}{1}</m>. This shows that the kernel
        of <m>\psi</m> is also trivial. 
      </p>
      <p>
        I have shown that any other injective map to a field can be
        realized as an injection to the fraction field and then
        another injection to the new field. This means that there is
        an isomorphic copy of the fraction field inside the other
        field. The fraction field is minimal: any other field that
        makes all elements of <m>R</m> invertible contains an
        isomorphism copy of the field of fractions as a subring.

      </p>
    </proof>
  </proposition>
</section>
